    <section class="card" id="s1">
      <h2>1. Application Purpose</h2>
      <p>
        Markov Quant Terminal is an educational analytical tool that uses historical daily stock prices
        to estimate market state, trend, risk, and possible price direction.
        The model combines a Markov model, trend model, combined score, risk panel,
        Monte Carlo simulation, and price chart.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s2">
      <h2>2. Input Data</h2>
      <p>
        The application uses daily closing prices.
        The variable <code>close[t]</code> means the closing price on day <code>t</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s3">
      <h2>3. Daily Return</h2>
      <p>Closing prices are converted into daily percentage returns.</p>
      <pre>return[t] = (close[t] - close[t-1]) / close[t-1]</pre>
      <p>If yesterday's price was 100 and today's price is 103:</p>
      <pre>(103 - 100) / 100 = 0.03 = 3 %</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s4">
      <h2>4. Markov States</h2>
      <p>Daily returns are divided into five states:</p>
      <pre>0 = Strong Bear
1 = Bear
2 = Neutral
3 = Bull
4 = Strong Bull</pre>

      <p>The model uses historical return quantiles:</p>
      <pre>q15 = 15th percentile
q40 = 40th percentile
q60 = 60th percentile
q85 = 85th percentile</pre>

      <p>Return classification:</p>
      <pre>if return &lt; q15  =&gt; Strong Bear
if return &lt; q40  =&gt; Bear
if return &lt; q60  =&gt; Neutral
if return &lt; q85  =&gt; Bull
else              =&gt; Strong Bull</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s5">
      <h2>5. Markov Matrix</h2>
      <p>The Markov matrix shows how often one state transitions into another state.</p>
      <pre>P[i][j] = number of transitions from state i to state j / total number of transitions from state i</pre>
      <p>
        <code>i</code> is the current state and <code>j</code> is the next state.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s6">
      <h2>6. Current Markov State</h2>
      <p>The current state is calculated from the last 20 classified days.</p>
      <pre>currentState = round(average(last 20 states))</pre>
      <p>
        If the last 20 days were mostly bullish, the current state will be closer to Bull or Strong Bull.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s7">
      <h2>7. Markov Signal</h2>
      <p>The current row of the Markov matrix is used to calculate down, neutral, and up probabilities.</p>
      <pre>down    = P[current][0] + P[current][1]
neutral = P[current][2]
up      = P[current][3] + P[current][4]</pre>

      <p>Signal logic:</p>
      <pre>if up &gt; down and up &gt; 0.52      =&gt; BUY
if down &gt; up and down &gt; 0.52    =&gt; SELL
else                             =&gt; NEUTRAL</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s8">
      <h2>8. Trend Model</h2>
      <p>The trend model measures price change over 20 and 60 days.</p>
      <pre>trend20 = (close[now] - close[now-20]) / close[now-20]
trend60 = (close[now] - close[now-60]) / close[now-60]</pre>

      <p>Trend signal logic:</p>
      <pre>if trend20 &gt; 0.03 and trend60 &gt; 0.06       =&gt; BULLISH
if trend20 &lt; -0.03 and trend60 &lt; -0.06     =&gt; BEARISH
else                                        =&gt; NEUTRAL</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s9">
      <h2>9. Combined Model</h2>
      <p>The Combined Model merges the Markov model, trend, and risk into one score.</p>
      <pre>score = 0
score += (up - down) * 2.2
score += (currentState - 2) * 0.35</pre>
      <p>
        If the upward probability is higher than the downward probability, the score increases.
        If the current state is above Neutral, the score also increases.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s10">
      <h2>10. Trend Contribution to Score</h2>
      <pre>if trend20 &gt; 0.03        score += 0.7
else if trend20 &gt; 0.01   score += 0.35
else if trend20 &lt; -0.03  score -= 0.7
else if trend20 &lt; -0.01  score -= 0.35

if trend60 &gt; 0.06        score += 0.8
else if trend60 &gt; 0.02   score += 0.4
else if trend60 &lt; -0.06  score -= 0.8
else if trend60 &lt; -0.02  score -= 0.4</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s11">
      <h2>11. Risk Adjustment</h2>
      <p>If risk is elevated, the score is reduced.</p>
      <pre>if crashRisk &gt; 0.12      score -= 0.4
if volatility &gt; 0.035    score -= 0.25</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s12">
      <h2>12. Combined Signal</h2>
      <p>The combined signal is determined from the score.</p>
      <pre>if score &gt; 0.75    =&gt; BUY
if score &lt; -0.75   =&gt; SELL
else               =&gt; NEUTRAL</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s13">
      <h2>13. Final Decision</h2>
      <p>Final Decision is the main output of the application. It is based on the Combined Signal and adjusted for risk.</p>
      <pre>finalSignal = combinedSignal

if riskLevel == HIGH and finalSignal == BUY:
    finalSignal = NEUTRAL</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s14">
      <h2>14. Risk Panel</h2>
      <p>
        The Risk Panel evaluates risk using two metrics:
        Crash Risk and Volatility.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s15">
      <h2>15. Crash Risk</h2>
      <p>Crash Risk measures how often the daily return was below -2 %.</p>
      <pre>crashRisk = number of days where return &lt; -0.02 / total number of days</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s16">
      <h2>16. Volatility</h2>
      <p>Volatility measures variability of daily returns.</p>
      <pre>avg = sum(return) / number of returns

volatility = sqrt( sum((return[t] - avg)^2) / number of returns )</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s17">
      <h2>17. Risk Level</h2>
      <p>Risk Level is determined using Crash Risk and volatility.</p>
      <pre>if crashRisk &gt; 0.14 or volatility &gt; 0.04   =&gt; HIGH
if crashRisk &gt; 0.08 or volatility &gt; 0.025  =&gt; MEDIUM
else                                        =&gt; LOW</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s18">
      <h2>18. Confidence</h2>
      <p>Confidence expresses the strength of the model output.</p>
      <pre>markovConfidence = max(up, down, neutral)

confidence = min(1, markovConfidence + abs(score) * 0.12)</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s19">
      <h2>19. Trend Strength</h2>
      <p>Trend Strength measures trend intensity using 20D and 60D movement.</p>
      <pre>trendStrength = min(1, abs(trend20) * 2.5 + abs(trend60) * 1.5)</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s20">
      <h2>20. Win Probability</h2>
      <p>Win Probability means the probability of transitioning into bullish Markov states.</p>
      <pre>winProbability = up

up = P[current][3] + P[current][4]</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s21">
      <h2>21. Monte Carlo Simulation</h2>
      <p>
        Monte Carlo simulation estimates possible future returns using historical average return and volatility.
      </p>
      <pre>simReturn = avg + volatility * random(-1, 1)

simTotal = sum(simReturn for N days)</pre>
      <p>
        The model runs 700 simulations for 30, 90, and 252 days.
        Outputs are the 5th percentile, median, and 95th percentile.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s22">
      <h2>22. Price Chart</h2>
      <p>
        The Price Chart shows historical closing prices.
        The chart is not a prediction. It only displays historical price movement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="s23">
      <h2>23. Markov Matrix</h2>
      <p>
        The Markov Matrix is a technical detail of the model.
        It shows transition probabilities between Strong Bear, Bear, Neutral, Bull, and Strong Bull states.
      </p>
    </section>

